WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:08.000
Hello everyone this is Peter Cooper from the
NCBI, welcome to today's webinar on standalone

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:16.080
BLAST 2.9.0 information relating to the database
5 format

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.190
This has various titles.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:21.990
A shorter one, we are going to talk mainly
about using organism information with standalone

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.050
BLAST+ (2.9.0).

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.220
And BLAST DBv5.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:31.070
This is a webinar I have already given almost
a year ago, but we wanted to update you on

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.890
some things about 5 and remind everybody and
to talk to those who did not hear us the first

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.720
time on what you can do with BLASTdbv5.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.620
So what we are going to do today is give a
little motivation behind why we made the changes,

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.860
talk about some features, show you how to
limit your search by taxonomy, search sequences

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.960
by accession faster and we will show you how
to retrieve sequences by taxonomy from a BLAST

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:56.960
database.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.540
It's useful to understand why we made the
changes.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:06.140
The main reason is to prepare for changes
that are coming to sequence identifiers, moving

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:13.770
to systems that use accession only sequence
identifiers rather than the integer IDs which

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:16.850
are called GIs.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:22.340
There are also changes in the protein chain
identifier coming from PDB, the structure

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.250
database

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:25.320
We need to make some changes in the database.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.880
That is all well and good and it sounds like
we are doing this because we have to do it

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.600
for our own purposes but you get things out
of this.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:41.580
One of the things you get is something people
have wanted for a long time, to have the taxonomic

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.530
information built-in to the BLAST databases.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.799
These make your organism searches much easier.

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:57.920
The database system we are using, LMDB enables
faster lookup of identifiers.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.340
You will also get access to proteins from
gi-less projects.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:09.009
We are now producing proteins that do not
have G.I.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:14.010
You cannot see those in the old BLAST databases
and when we get the new PDB records with the

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:18.550
long chain IDs, you will be able to access
those in the BLAST databases.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.540
Just let me point out something.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.629
All of the software relies on integer identifiers,
there are various ones for our databases.

00:02:25.629 --> 00:02:32.420
The ones for the sequence databases are called
GI numbers, they are integers and all systems

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.349
have traditionally depended on these identifiers.

00:02:35.349 --> 00:02:43.730
These are basically ints in terms of program
language so there is a limit depending on

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.730
if they are signed or unsigned of 2 billion
up to 4 billion.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:55.859
The thing I want to point out is we are already
at about 1 billion GIs so that means we are

00:02:55.859 --> 00:02:57.989
going to run out.

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:04.169
so this will be a problem and we will have
to rewrite a lot of software so we will move

00:03:04.169 --> 00:03:09.739
away from using GIs and using accession dot
versions instead.

00:03:09.739 --> 00:03:16.150
And because the number of GIs we have left
is somewhat limited, we are kind of rationing.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:21.629
For some projects, in particular the whole
genome shotgun projects that are coming in

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:27.139
now that we do make proteins, we have stopped
assigning G.I. numbers.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:34.779
so there is a whole bunch of these that you
can see in the sequence set browser.

00:03:34.779 --> 00:03:40.639
Nucleotide and protein sequences that do not
have GI numbers so you cannot find these by

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:46.669
searching the resources by using ordinary
queries, you can find them by the identifiers

00:03:46.669 --> 00:03:52.189
and these proteins in particular will not
be present in the v4 blast databases, the

00:03:52.189 --> 00:03:53.930
old versions.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:59.760
Just to point out, many of these proteins
are coming from pathogen detection projects

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.459
which is collaborative between a number of
government agencies involved in identifying

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:09.010
and analyzing foodborne illness outbreak.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:13.129
This is one of the major sources of gi-less
proteins and these are important because the

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:18.840
organisms are important and so are the proteins
so we need to have access to those and that's

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.229
why we need the new BLAST version and the
new database.

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:33.150
So what has been up for a while at NCBI is
sort of not quite a fully DBv5 database which

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.140
means it does have GI numbers.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:42.889
In order to work with the fully gi-less database
you will have to upgrade both the BLAST binary

00:04:42.889 --> 00:04:46.060
and get the new DBv5.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:52.620
Executables for 2.9.0 are available in the
usual place on the BLAST ftp directory and

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:57.380
the /LATEST/ link in the ftp directory.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:03.800
2.9.0 will work with version 4 but I want
to point out we will stop updating version

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:11.240
4 eventually and we are thinking about stopping
after September 2019.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:17.230
In order to work with the newest databases
which are not yet on the FTP site, you will

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:20.130
need to upgrade to the 2.9.0.

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:26.120
The updated GI-less databases for version
5 should be on the FTP site we are thinking

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:27.120
May 20.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.509
And I have a little footnote although it's
not something I want to advertise as a great

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:37.139
thing, a side effect of using these on the
web is you will notice you cannot use Entrez

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:38.139
queries.

00:05:38.139 --> 00:05:42.210
the organism ones are still there and that's
what most people need.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.690
So speaking of that, the thing I always tell
people when they do a BLAST search on the

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:53.520
web is most importantly limit by organism
and you can do that by typing the organism

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:59.430
name in the bottom of the BLAST form where
it says organism to include or exclude.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:07.680
In standalone BLAST the way in the past it
would be by gi list and that will not work

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.800
anymore with version 5 databases.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.990
So in order to do this you can do some other
things.

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:21.310
They do not require using GIs, one is using
the seqidlist.

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:28.909
Those can be accessions, so you can use accession.version
format to limit to a set of sequences in the

00:06:28.909 --> 00:06:30.289
database.

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:36.780
You can also exclude using a negative list,
again that will have the accessions you don't

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.780
want to see.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:42.789
And then there are a set of things that have
to do with the baked in taxonomy in the database.

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:50.560
So you can search for a particular tax ID
or a small set that you can pass as an argument,

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:55.169
or pass as a negative argument so you can
exclude certain groups.

00:06:55.169 --> 00:07:00.810
Or you can upload a file of taxids to either
include or exclude.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:08.580
These things work on search programs for blastdbcmd
, the program used to extract things from

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:14.469
the databases, you can retrieve sequences
for particular taxids or using blastdbcmd.

00:07:14.469 --> 00:07:24.669
Looking at any of the databases that are 5
format, you can get -tax_info to look at what

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:30.530
is in there and how many sequences each taxa
has.

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:38.930
So how do you get a list of taxids ? You can
do that on the web easily, so you can go to

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:44.550
the taxonomy database at NCBI and search for
example a set of organisms so I'm getting

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:47.539
all taxids for green plants in this case.

00:07:47.539 --> 00:07:52.460
green plants[subtree] You can download that
or I could do exclusion if I want a particular

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:57.319
set of taxids in this case for green plants
without the monocots,

00:07:57.319 --> 00:08:00.860
green plants[subtree] NOT liliopsida[subtree]
You can create a file and download it and

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:03.110
use that to limit your search.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:08.490
In this case this is about 209,000 taxids
which is not too bad you could download in

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.069
a few seconds.

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:15.870
You could also do it from the convenience
of your command line with a shell script that

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:22.889
comes with the blast release and it is get_species_taxids.sh,
a shell program.

00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:27.300
You can either input a name and get back the
ID or you can input a taxid to get all the

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.330
taxids included in that taxon.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:36.140
This uses our EDirect package so that has
to be installed and for that reason only works

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:40.389
for Unix Linux or Cygwin.

00:08:40.389 --> 00:08:45.850
So you don't have to use those, you could
do it on the web, but this is pretty convenient.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:52.660
